# .mutation-allowlist - EMPTY ON PURPOSE.
#
# There are no entries, and adding one is a decision to make, not a formality.
#
# An entry used to be the way to silence a surviving mutant. It is the worst tool
# available here, for three reasons that this file now exists to prevent:
#
#   1. It is keyed by LINE NUMBER. Code moves inside a file without changing what
#      it does, so a single translation silently retired all 34 entries at once:
#      16 past the end of their file, the rest pointing at unrelated lines. A
#      stale entry does not fail, it just sits there looking like calibration.
#   2. An entry whose justification is "this boundary is unreachable" is a claim
#      about the code that nobody re-checks. Half of them turned out to be false
#      when measured: three survivors that had been filed as equivalent were
#      observable after all, and were plain gaps in the suite.
#   3. It reads as rigour. A file of entries with careful prose looks like the
#      mutation gate is doing work, and hides the fact that those lines are
#      precisely the ones no test reaches.
#
# So a surviving mutant has two honest answers, and only two:
#
#   - REFACTOR so the mutant cannot be produced. A boundary comparison whose
#     equality case is excluded by a guard is not a test gap, it is the wrong
#     shape: `cmp.Or(cmp.Compare(a, b), ...)` has no operator to mutate, and
#     `x[max(0, len(x)-n):]` has no boundary to argue about. Where a slice is cut
#     to its own whole length, the branch was a no-op and the total expression
#     says the same thing without a comparison.
#   - TEST it. If the boundary is observable, the suite should say so. Drive it
#     through the public path, and assert the EXPECTED value written out: a test
#     that checks the output is "coherent with itself" also passes with an
#     inverted comparator, because an inverted order is coherent too.
#
# Before believing that a survivor is equivalent, measure it. Hand-apply the
# mutation and paste the failing test; if nothing fails, sweep the reachable
# inputs and look for one where the two forms differ. Twice during the work that
# produced this file, a boundary assumed unreachable turned out to differ on
# thousands of real inputs.
#
# Note that gremlins does not report TIMED OUT mutants at all, so they cannot be
# allowlisted and cannot be detected: a run on a loaded machine reports a SUBSET
# of the survivors, never a superset. Read the timeout count before believing
# any survivor count.
#
# This file is kept, present and empty, on purpose: the Gate step skips itself
# when this file is MISSING, which would leave the whole mutation job reporting
# results without blocking on anything.